Vietnam experienced exceptionally atypical weather and disaster patterns in 2025, resulting in significant damage across the country, according to Hoang Duc Cuong, Deputy Director of the Hydrometeorological Department. Deputy Minister Phung Duc Tien also noted that the nation might still face its 16th storm of the year as 2025 draws to a close.

A notable event occurred in October when the Bach Ma station in Thua Thien Hue recorded an extraordinary 1,739 mm of rainfall within a single day. The World Meteorological Organization (WMO) is currently assessing this figure for potential acknowledgment as one of the greatest 24-hour rainfall totals ever documented worldwide.

The East Sea saw an unprecedented surge in storm and tropical depression occurrences, marking the highest activity level since Vietnam began record-keeping in 1961. By the close of November, a total of 21 storms and tropical depressions had been documented, surpassing the previous high of 20 established in 2017. The 2025 storm season was marked by several uncommon and severe events, including Storm No. 1 (WUTIP) emerging in the East Sea in June, an occurrence not witnessed in over four decades. Additionally, a tropical depression transitioned from the Indian Ocean to the northwestern Pacific in late November, marking an unprecedented path. Storm No. 9 (RAGASA) achieved level 17 wind speeds, with gusts surpassing this level, making it the most powerful storm ever documented in the East Sea.

Flooding in 2025 was notably extensive and intense, with maximum river heights exceeding past historical benchmarks across numerous areas. These floods stretched from the Northern and Central territories to the Central Highlands, further impacting the lower Mekong Delta. For the first time in a single year, 20 rivers recorded either extreme or historic flooding, encompassing rivers such as the Cau River (Thai Nguyen, Bac Ninh), the Thuong River (Bac Giang), the Bang Giang River (Cao Bang), and the Lo River (Tuyen Quang). Significantly, the historic flood in eastern Dak Lak, once a section of Phu Yen province, surpassed the 1993 flood peak levels on the Ba River basin.

The array of atypical occurrences in Vietnam and globally during 2025 indicates a shift in conventional flood patterns, with the pattern of intensifying weather extremes becoming more evident, directly impacted by climate change.
