COLOMBO – Sri Lankan officials on Sunday contended with escalating floodwaters affecting sections of the capital, Colombo, particularly its northern region. The severe flooding follows a week of intense rainfall caused by formidable Cyclone Ditwah, which departed Sri Lanka on Saturday. Although rainfall has diminished throughout the island, intense upstream rainfall continues to cause flooding in low-lying regions adjacent to the Kelani River, whose water level kept climbing.

The Disaster Management Centre (DMC) reported that the number of fatalities had climbed to 159, with an additional 203 individuals unaccounted for. Numerous families remain confined or stranded in their homes due to the rising floodwaters in Colombo.

Close to 200,000 individuals have been rescued and relocated to shelters. Over 122,000 individuals are now residing in government-operated temporary shelters, with an additional 833,000 needing aid after being uprooted by the floods. Over 20,000 homes have been devastated or lie in ruins due to the severe weather system.

Infrastructure has been significantly impacted. Approximately one-third of the nation still lacks electricity and access to running water due to downed power lines and flooded water treatment plants. Authorities confirm power and communication services are expected to resume in three days. Access to the internet has also faced interruptions, and numerous roads in the hardest-hit central province continue to be impassable.

In response to the crisis, President Anura Kumara Dissanayake announced a state of emergency on Saturday. Military personnel from the army, navy, and air force have been dispatched to work in conjunction with civilian workers and volunteers, contributing to relief efforts. The government has sought assistance from the international community to address the cyclone's aftermath.

International aid has begun to arrive. India promptly dispatched relief supplies, two helicopters, and their crews for rescue operations. Japan announced plans to dispatch a team to evaluate urgent requirements and committed to providing additional support.

This cyclone marks Sri Lanka's most lethal natural catastrophe since 2017, when floods and landslides resulted in over 200 fatalities and displaced hundreds of thousands. The most severe flooding since the beginning of the 21st century occurred in June 2003, claiming 254 lives.
