The environment ministry reported on Tuesday that since Saturday, over 8,600 individuals across four central provinces have sought refuge in schools and other public facilities due to severe flooding and landslides. Vietnam's central coastal region has been deluged with heavy rain since the weekend, leading to school closures in Hue and Danang. Flooding also caused disruptions on the railway connecting the northern and southern parts of the country. In Hoi An, state media captured images of tourists maneuvering boats through the town's narrow lanes, while AFP journalists reported on evacuation efforts in severely inundated areas.

Experts attribute the increased lethality and destruction of extreme weather phenomena, including storms and floods, to climate change driven by human activities. Earlier in October, Vietnam's rugged northern regions and its capital, Hanoi, experienced severe flooding caused by typhoons Bualoi and Matmo.
