A remarkably intense storm, Kalmaegi, struck in November, churning over the South China Sea as it neared Vietnam after exiting the Philippines on Wednesday. Its winds consistently blew at approximately 114 mph, with gusts potentially climbing to 137 mph.

In the central Philippines, Kalmaegi claimed the lives of at least 114 individuals and left 127 unaccounted for, marking the most devastating natural catastrophe to strike the nation this year. An additional 69 individuals suffered injuries. President Ferdinand Marcos Jr. announced a state of emergency on Thursday, enabling the government to expedite the release of emergency funds and curb food hoarding and excessive pricing. The typhoon forced the displacement of over 560,000 villagers, with nearly 450,000 of them relocated to emergency shelters.

The central province of Cebu bore the brunt of the storm's fury. State forecaster Benison Estareja reported that Kalmaegi unleashed a deluge equivalent to one-and-a-half months of precipitation in merely one day on Tuesday across metropolitan Cebu. Heavy rains triggered flash floods, swelling a river and other waterways in Cebu city and its neighboring towns. Torrential floodwaters engulfed or carried off numerous vehicles in residential areas, compelling inhabitants to seek refuge on their rooftops, where they frantically called for help.

At least 71 fatalities occurred in Cebu, primarily attributed to drowning incidents. In the Cebu town of Liloan, Krizza Espra visited the mortuary on Thursday, where the remains of her husband and three children, who perished when their roof caved in, were prepared for a wake. Four other family members, including her mother and aunt, remained unaccounted for. Authorities also noted that an additional 62 individuals were unaccounted for in the central province of Negros Occidental, close to Cebu.

Governor Pamela Baricuatro conveyed that while all possible measures were taken for the typhoon, unforeseen events like flash floods can still occur. Ms. Baricuatro stated that the storm's impacts might have been aggravated by quarrying activities leading to river blockage and overflow, combined with inadequate flood control measures in Cebu province. A scandal involving corruption in inadequate or missing flood control initiatives throughout the Philippines has ignited public anger and prompted street demonstrations in recent months. Furthermore, Cebu continues to grapple with the aftermath of a 6.9 magnitude earthquake that struck on September 30, resulting in at least 79 fatalities and the collapse or extensive damage of numerous homes.

Six fatalities linked to Kalmaegi occurred when a Philippine military helicopter crashed in the southern province of Agusan del Sur on Tuesday. The crew was en route to deliver humanitarian aid to areas heavily impacted by the typhoon.

Vietnam's central provinces are currently experiencing severe flooding due to record-breaking rains, with Kalmaegi predicted to deliver over 24 inches of rain in certain regions. In coastal cities like Danang, waves up to 10ft high battered the coast and robust winds toppled trees. Numerous residences in Quy Nhon experienced prolonged power outages. Ho Chi Minh City, the nation's economic center, confronts an increased threat of significant flooding, and high tides were anticipated on the Saigon River. Officials cautioned that as much as 4 inches of rain might flood regions at lower elevations.

Disaster response officials have issued a warning that an approaching tropical cyclone from the Pacific may intensify into a super typhoon, potentially striking the northern Philippines at the start of next week.
