The Syrian National Earthquake Center participated in an advanced workshop held in Algeria on November 11-12. The event, orchestrated by the Arab Center for Earthquake and Natural Disaster Prevention, focused on evaluating earthquake hazards and instructing attendees on sophisticated systems. It successfully convened specialists from ten Arab nations and partnered with the Global Earthquake Model (GEM) Foundation.

Participants received training on the OpenQuake Engine software, learning to craft detailed seismic hazard models and generate risk maps to evaluate potential earthquake effects. Jamal Zakkour, who leads the National Earthquake Center, remarked that the workshop offered significant insights into how Algeria assesses the risk of earthquakes. He highlighted that the training specifically encompassed developing accurate models to evaluate earthquake hazards and their possible impacts.

Zakkour also noted that the event facilitated the sharing of expertise and advanced methods among the involved nations, enhancing comprehension of seismic phenomena in the area and bolstering disaster response plans. Syria's involvement is consistent with its initiatives to bolster scientific readiness as part of the Ministry of Emergency and Disaster Management's strategy.

Established in 2004, the Arab Center for Earthquake and Natural Disaster Prevention aims to enhance regional collaboration in the fields of earthquake science and the mitigation of natural disaster risks. From its inception, the center has focused on enhancing regional readiness by fostering advanced scientific education and collaborative efforts.
