data.fused_content:
Confirmed fatalities across Sri Lanka have reached at least 334 individuals following a devastating cyclone. Approximately 400 individuals remain unaccounted for, including five Navy personnel who were last observed attempting to stem the flow of excess water at a Naval sub-station located in Chalai Lagoon, in the northeastern part of the country.

Authorities cautioned that additional intense precipitation linked to Cyclone Ditwah is anticipated throughout the island country in the upcoming days. Even as the cyclone itself has moved on, upstream heavy rains are currently causing flooding in low-lying areas along the Kelani River, which has seen escalating water levels. The northern area of Colombo is grappling with severe flooding.

The severe weather system has resulted in the destruction of nearly 15,000 homes nationwide, leading approximately 44,000 individuals to seek refuge in government-operated emergency shelters, as reported by the Disaster Management Centre (DMC). Overall, the extreme weather system has demolished more than 20,000 homes, displacing 122,000 individuals to reside in government-operated temporary shelters. An additional 833,000 people required assistance after being uprooted by the floods.

President Anura Kumara Dissanayake announced a state of emergency on Saturday, addressing the consequences following the cyclone and calling for global support. India promptly dispatched relief goods along with two helicopters and their crews to conduct rescue operations. Japan announced plans to dispatch a team to evaluate urgent requirements and committed to providing additional aid.

Over 24,000 personnel from the police, army, and air force remain engaged in efforts to reach families isolated by the floodwaters. Military personnel from the army, navy, and air force are working in conjunction with civilian workers and volunteers to support the relief operations.

Al Jazeera’s Minelle Fernandez, reporting from Sammanthurai in the east central region of Sri Lanka, noted that the nation is facing challenges in managing the cyclone's destructive aftermath. She observed entire neighborhoods submerged under layers of mud, each adding to a growing sense of hopelessness. Fernandez remarked that the lines of communication have failed, with certain regions still unable to relay updates. In other areas, the incessant rainfall has led to newly planted rice crops being inundated with water.

Nearly one-third of the nation is still without power and running water, with inundated water purification facilities. Internet connectivity has similarly been compromised. Numerous roads in the heavily impacted central province continue to be impassable.

This cyclone marks Sri Lanka's most lethal natural disaster since 2017, when floods and landslides resulted in the deaths of over 200 individuals and forced hundreds of thousands to relocate. The worst flooding since the turn of the century occurred in June 2003, resulting in the loss of 254 lives.

Separately, the death toll in Indonesia has climbed to 303 following devastating floods in Sumatra.
