The Labor government is set to allocate $200 million towards 96 projects across the nation, aimed at bolstering community resilience against severe weather conditions. This funding marks the third round of financial support from the $1 billion Disaster Ready Fund (DRF), a governmental program dedicated to enhancing disaster resilience and mitigating risks.

Over half of the $200 million federal investment will be allocated to infrastructure projects, with some designated for immediate construction initiation. Approved initiatives include establishing recovery centers, flood protection infrastructure, reducing the dangers posed by bushfires, and implementing sophisticated alert systems for natural threats.

Specific projects include the construction of an 800-person capacity cyclone shelter and evacuation center on Palm Island, off the coast of Queensland, an area highly vulnerable to tropical cyclones and severe weather events. Additional initiatives feature a $1.6 million infrastructure undertaking aimed at safeguarding Townsvale Estate in South Australia, a $14.1 million investment for enhancing stormwater infrastructure in Narrandera, Central NSW, and $895,000 to enhance the capacity of Karajarri Rangers, focusing on reducing severe wildfire threats in northwest WA. Flood mitigation strategies in Launceston are also part of the plan.

In earlier rounds, the DRF allocated approximately $400 million in federal funds to over 350 projects. Almost 50% of these projects are currently in the pre-construction or construction-delivery stages, with 43 projects already approaching completion or finalized, according to the department. This third round elevates the total investment through the DRF to $600 million, with numerous project backers matching the federal funding. The third round will benefit every one of the 538 local government areas spanning all states and territories.

Emergency Management Minister Kristy McBain stated, "Australians are well-acquainted with the annual onslaught of floods, bushfires, and cyclones, which is precisely why the Albanese government initiated the Disaster Ready Fund, allocating up to $1 billion over the span of five years."
