Government officials at both federal and Victorian levels are intensifying their support for families and primary producers affected by January's bushfires in Victoria, building upon previous disaster recovery initiatives. This fresh assistance complements existing Commonwealth–State Disaster Recovery Funding Arrangements and initial aid from the Personal Hardship Assistance Program.

Premier Jacinta Allan, during a visit to the severely impacted community of Natimuk, announced that uninsured homeowners who have lost their main residence will be eligible for grants up to $52,250. Allan stated that the devastation wrought by these fires has been "beyond measure," prompting the provision of additional financial and psychological aid to alleviate challenges faced by farmers and regional communities.

Federal Minister for Emergency Management, Kristy McBain, affirmed the Albanese government's close collaboration with Victoria during the recovery phase. McBain stated, "We want the people of Victoria to be assured that the Albanese government supports you not only in responding to these fires but also throughout the recovery process."

Assistance initiatives are concentrating on improving access and support for farmers and livestock. The Victorian government is enhancing mental health and business aid for farmers, particularly those whose crops and livestock have been affected. The "Look Over the Farm Gate" initiative, managed by the National Centre for Farmer Health, is scheduled to commence on January 16. It will provide grants of up to $5,000 for local events and activities in farming areas to support mental health and community recovery. Counsellors active during earlier drought periods are also set to visit impacted farms or connect with farming families by phone. Additionally, the Rural Financial Counselling Service will provide complimentary, confidential wellbeing assistance, coupled with financial and business counselling.

Animal welfare remains a pressing issue for farmers unable to safely revisit their lands. Acting Victorian Minister for Agriculture, Steve Dimopoulos, stated that efforts, in collaboration with Agriculture Victoria and the Fire Incident Controller, are focused on simplifying the process for farmers to monitor their animals' well-being, acknowledging the distressing nature of this period. Property owners may submit requests to Agriculture Victoria to determine the timing and method for inspecting their animals.

To facilitate public donations, the Victorian government is initiating the 2026 Victorian Bushfire Appeal. Victorian Minister for Natural Disaster Recovery, Vicki Ward, explained that the appeal aims to distribute funds based on priorities set by local communities, in collaboration with charities and community organisations, ensuring generous donations reach where they are most needed. The government has assured that all contributions will be allocated to communities impacted by the fires, with the state covering administrative expenses.

Simultaneously, insurance providers are noting an uptick in claims volumes. According to the Insurance Council of Australia (ICA), insurers have received 1,386 claims since January 7, spanning home, commercial, motor, and business interruption policies. Preliminary data indicates that approximately 30% of these property claims account for total losses.

In response to the ongoing bushfires and extreme weather, general insurers are activating catastrophe protocols. As access to affected regions improves, insurers have commenced claim processing and are dispatching personnel to these areas. ICA staff and several insurers are assisting policyholders with lodging claims and addressing queries from the Castlemaine Recovery Centre, with plans for further outreach in additional communities. Insurers are managing in-person claims, organizing temporary housing, and facilitating emergency financial aid, especially for those experiencing power failures.

IAG has established a mobile claims unit in Seymour to assist patrons of its affiliated brands. IAG's Major Event Response Teams have initiated visits to properties in affected neighborhoods to conduct evaluations and undertake urgent repairs. From its Disaster Management Centre in Brisbane, IAG employs satellite imagery to assess damage in regions where ground access remains limited. Suncorp has also activated its extreme weather protocol in response to the Victorian bushfires and Ex-Tropical Cyclone Koji, registering approximately 80 home and motor claims associated with Ex-Tropical Cyclone Koji and roughly 60 claims related to the Victorian bushfires by 6pm AEST on January 11.

Health funds are revising their policies for clients in disaster-stricken areas. HCF has rolled out a relief initiative for members affected by the Victorian bushfires and other severe weather occurrences. This initiative offers eligible members a premium waiver of up to three months, the ability to pause coverage for up to two years (with no claims applicable during this period), and participation in the HealthyMinds Check-in program, which offers private consultations with certified psychologists, encompassing support for teenagers. HCF is also providing qualified members in rural and remote locations with complimentary 15-minute online GP consultations via its collaboration with GP2U, ensuring appointments are accessible every day of the week. For insurers and intermediaries, the current bushfire season is emphasizing disaster response abilities, managing a surge in claims, and working alongside government initiatives and health and rural support services as recovery and rebuilding efforts continue over the upcoming months.
