The death toll from floods and landslides on Sumatra has risen to 174, with approximately 80 individuals remaining missing, a disaster official reported. Fatalities include 116 in North Sumatra, 25 in Aceh, and 23 bodies discovered in West Sumatra, according to the National Disaster Management Agency.

Thousands of homes were inundated across affected regions, with over 17,000 homes flooded in West Sumatra and more than 3,200 homes and structures affected in North Sumatra province. Many homes were submerged up to their roofs, and around 23,000 people in West Sumatra were compelled to seek refuge in temporary shelters. The floods and landslides also destroyed rice fields, livestock, and public facilities, severing bridges and roads and isolating residents.

Torrential monsoon rains triggered the disaster, causing rivers in North Sumatra province to overflow and sending mud and rocks crashing into hilly settlements. The extreme weather was driven by tropical cyclone Senyar, which formed in the Strait of Malacca. Senyar heightened rainfall, fierce winds, and elevated waves in Aceh, North Sumatra, West Sumatra, Riau, and adjacent areas before fading.

Achadi Subarkah Raharjo of Indonesia’s Meteorology, Climatology, and Geophysical Agency explained that the extreme weather warning has been extended due to a robust water vapor supply and changing atmospheric dynamics. He cautioned that ongoing unstable atmospheric conditions could prolong the extreme weather while the cyclone system remains active. The continuous downpours have rendered the steep, saturated terrain extremely susceptible to disasters.

In Indonesia, an archipelago with 17,000 islands, seasonal rains often trigger flooding and landslides, impacting millions residing in mountainous regions or near fertile flood plains.
