COLOMBO - Sri Lanka declared a state of emergency on Saturday as fatalities from floods and landslides induced by Cyclone Ditwah reached 153, with 191 individuals unaccounted for. The Disaster Management Centre (DMC) reported that severe weather conditions devastated over 20,000 homes, displacing 798,000 people and forcing 108,000 into government-run temporary shelters. DMC spokesman Pradeep Kodippili confirmed the death toll.

Army, navy, and air force personnel, alongside civilian workers and volunteers, have been mobilized for relief operations. A passenger, recounting from a hospital, described how navy sailors used ropes to guide them safely through floodwaters to the roof of a nearby house. An initial helicopter rescue attempt was canceled when its rotor downdraft risked dislodging the roof they were on, with naval boats subsequently completing the rescue.

The central district of Badulla was particularly hard-hit, with roads remaining impassable, isolating several villages and blocking relief supplies. Approximately a third of the country experienced power outages and lacked running water due to collapsed power lines and inundated water purification facilities. Saman Kumara, a resident of Maspanna in Badulla, reported, "We lost two people in our village others have taken refuge in a temple and one house still standing. We can't leave the village, and no one can enter as all routes are obstructed by landslides. We lack food and are running out of clean water." Internet connections were also disrupted across affected areas.

Cyclone Ditwah departed the island on Saturday, heading towards neighboring India. In response, Chennai Airport in India canceled 54 flights, as meteorologists predicted extremely heavy rainfall and strong winds for the next 48 hours.

