Emergency shelters have been established in Victoria and Saanich, triggering an Extreme Weather Response (EWR) due to frigid temperatures and strong winds. Environment Canada predicts temperatures approaching freezing, with overnight lows expected around 1 C and even chillier conditions further inland. Winds are forecast to increase to 30 kilometres per hour, with gusts possibly reaching 70 kilometres per hour near the Juan de Fuca Strait, before easing overnight.

In Saanich, an Extreme Weather Response shelter, managed by Our Place Society, has been activated. This initiative receives financial backing from the District of Saanich, provided by BC Housing. Broadview United Church offers 20 overnight beds. Additionally, a facility at 3703 St. Aidan’s St., also managed by Our Place Society, features 25 provisional winter shelter spots and 25 fixed sleeping accommodations. Saanich residents also have the option to utilize the shelter services provided by the Victoria Native Friendship Centre, which accommodates 50 individuals.

These EWR spaces become operational solely upon the anticipation of dangerous weather conditions and are distinct from regular nightly shelter accommodations. The spaces will remain accessible as long as severe weather conditions satisfy provincial activation standards, which may encompass freezing temperatures, prolonged rain, or prolonged snowfall. Saanich has also made daytime public warming facilities available. Residents are welcome to seek warmth at municipal recreation centers and the Saanich municipal hall during their standard hours of operation. The available facilities include Saanich Commonwealth Place, Gordon Head Recreation Centre, Cedar Hill Recreation and Arts Centre, and G.R. Pearkes Recreation Centre. The district notes that operational hours might fluctuate in response to severe weather, influenced by staffing availability.

In Victoria, the city reports the addition of 30 more shelter mats under the Emergency Weather Response initiative. Collaborators at the Salvation Army ARC have increased their capacity to provide these additional mats. Overnight accommodations are accessible at 525 Johnson St, beginning at 8 p.m. on Friday and concluding at 7 a.m. on Saturday. The facility accommodates individuals of all genders and has a strict no-pet policy.
