North Sumatra has been battered by severe weather for several days, with the national disaster agency BNPB reporting flooding in parts of the Tapanuli Selatan district since Monday.

In Tapanuli Selatan, floods and landslides have claimed the lives of eight individuals, injured 58, and led to the evacuation of 2,851 residents, according to BNPB representative Abdul Muhari. The local disaster agency has dispatched heavy machinery to remove debris obstructing road access to the district. At least three other districts within the province have also experienced the impact of floods and landslides.

The Meteorology, Climatology, and Geophysics Agency attributes the extreme weather to cyclone seeds, which are weather formations capable of developing into tropical cyclones. The yearly monsoon period, generally spanning from June to September, frequently results in intense rainfall, leading to landslides, flash floods, and the spread of water-borne diseases.

Climate change has altered storm patterns, affecting both the length and strength of the season, resulting in increased rainfall, more frequent flash floods, and intensified wind gusts.

This month, landslides instigated by intense rainfall have claimed the lives of at least 38 individuals in Central Java, with approximately 13 others still unaccounted for.
