JAKARTA – Fatalities from severe storms across three Indonesian provinces on Sumatra have risen to 442, with 402 individuals still unaccounted for, as the repercussions of intense weather across Southeast Asia have escalated throughout the past week. Approximately 1.1 million individuals have been impacted, with 290,000 compelled to leave their residences.

North Sumatra bore the brunt, experiencing torrential downpours since November 25 as a result of Cyclone Senyar and Cyclone Koto, which are uncommon meteorological events in the Sumatra area. West Sumatra and Aceh also faced substantial rainfall. The severe flooding has similarly impacted Southern Thailand, northern Malaysia, and Vietnam, with the total death toll across the broader region exceeding 700. These events are largely being linked to climate change.

Landslides obstructing access routes in highland regions have impeded both evacuations and the delivery of aid. Suhariyanto, head of Indonesia's National Disaster Mitigation Agency (BNPB), noted that certain areas remain inaccessible, with signs suggesting further casualties. Extended power outages and disrupted communication systems have further hindered rescue and relief teams. In response, electric generators and Starlink units have been supplied to local authorities, evacuation facilities, and military and police personnel.

Hery Marantika, who leads the Search and Rescue Office in Medan, North Sumatra, stated that erratic weather patterns and unstable soil structure pose significant difficulties. Rianda Purba of the Indonesian Forum for the Environment (Walhi) in North Sumatra highlighted that Central Tapanuli, a severely affected regency, is situated along the slopes of the Bukit Barisan mountain ridge. Land cover in the upstream and riverbank zones of this area has markedly diminished, primarily due to deforestation linked to the Batang Toru hydropower project, local logging operations, and gold mining activities.

The Meteorology, Climatology, and Geophysics Agency (BMKG) had issued a warning last Wednesday regarding the possible effects of tropical cyclones Senyar and Koto on three provinces, including Riau and the Riau Islands. Dwikorita Karnawati, Chairwoman of BMKG, stated that tropical cyclones have seen an increase in frequency over the last decade, typically occurring from November until March or April. She attributed the rising occurrence of cyclones to climate change, noting that Indonesia's broad swathes of susceptible regions, especially its elevated terrains and mountainous areas, are typically the first to experience the impacts of hydrometeorological disasters.

The central government has refrained from announcing a national disaster, leaving the determination of emergency status to the discretion of individual provincial or regency governments. Suhariyanto stated that Indonesia has exclusively recognized the COVID-19 pandemic and the 2004 tsunami as national disasters, despite the storms impacting three provinces collectively home to over 20 million people. Evacuation efforts across the three provinces are spearheaded by BNPB, alongside the National Search and Rescue Agency, the military, and the police. President Prabowo Subianto stated on Friday that the government responded promptly, delivering assistance both by land and through aerial means, despite very difficult conditions with many roads blocked and adverse weather preventing helicopters and planes from landing. The central government dispatched three Hercules C-130 aircraft alongside an A-400 aircraft, all loaded with emergency provisions for impacted residents.

