A lightning flash extending 515 miles from Texas to Missouri has been recognized as the longest electrical bolt in the world, according to an August 5 press release from the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration (NOAA). The World Meteorological Organization (WMO) set this new benchmark, classifying the event as a "megaflash" due to its extremely long duration and distance.

An analysis of a satellite image from NOAA's GOES-16 series uncovered that this lightning strike, which occurred on October 22, 2017, surpassed the length of a previous record-holder by approximately 38 miles. The previous record, measuring 477 miles, was believed to have occurred on April 29, 2020, spanning across Texas, Louisiana, and Mississippi. Both the 2017 and 2020 megaflashes took place within a recognized "storm hotspot" in the Great Plains area of the United States.

The WMO reported that the unprecedented 2017 lightning bolt originated within a significant thunderstorm system. The stretch from eastern Texas to near Kansas City mirrors the span from Paris to Venice in Europe. A car journey would last around eight to nine hours, while a commercial flight would require at least 90 minutes to traverse this distance.

Professor Randall Cerveny, featured in Weather and Climate Extremes for the WMO, noted that recent progress in science and technology now enables the evaluation of extreme weather conditions, including the measurement of lightning distances. Cerveny remarked, "This new record clearly demonstrates the incredible power of the natural environment," adding, "It is likely that even greater extremes still exist."

The NOAA GOES-16 satellite, part of the Geostationary Operational Environmental Satellite program, captured images of both megaflashes. Launched in 2016, GOES-16 entered its geostationary orbit and marked the beginning of the GOES-R satellite series, which comprises four satellites, with the last launched in June 2024.

The United States experiences approximately 25 million cloud-to-ground lightning strikes each year, associated with 24,000 fatalities and 240,000 injuries. To enhance weather prediction and comprehension, the U.S. government, via NOAA and NASA, conducts ongoing monitoring and research on lightning activity.

The National Centers for Environmental Information (NCEI) within NOAA monitors the financial impact of extreme weather events, which amounts to billions of dollars. According to NCEI's website, 2023 saw a record 28 separate billion-dollar events. In 2024, they documented 27 significant natural disasters, including 17 severe storms, 5 tropical cyclones, and 2 winter storm/cold waves, which collectively incurred costs totaling $182.7 billion. These costly occurrences also include wildfires, droughts, and heatwaves. In January, NCEI announced that the financial impact of natural disasters in the U.S. this year could increase by several billion dollars relative to 2024.

Meteorological forecasts predict that anticipated rises in severe weather incidents, elevated temperatures, and various shifting climate patterns are likely to lead to more intense storms.
