Calgary's hailstorms last year resulted in insured damages nearing $2.8 billion, marking it as the second priciest natural disaster in Canadian history, according to the Insurance Bureau of Canada (IBC). Both meteorologists and insurers observe a concerning pattern: hail occurrences in Alberta are becoming increasingly frequent, intense, and costly for homeowners and the insurance sector. Alberta currently leads the nation in both claim frequency and expense due to hail damage.

In response to these conditions, Class 4 impact-resistant shingles are gaining popularity. These shingles are engineered to endure the impact of large hailstones and other harsh weather conditions. Mike Payne, owner of Calgary-based No Payne Roofing, stated, "At No Payne, our installations feature only Class 4 shingles due to their hail-resistant and hail-proof qualities, which ensure long-lasting durability and minimize damage to homes in Calgary." While Class 4 shingles might eventually become a building code standard, they currently offer a practical way to save time, money, and the inconvenience of insurance claims.

Despite the robust surface protection offered by Class 4 shingles, the quality of the underlying structure is equally crucial. Many older homes in Calgary feature plywood roof decking installed over four decades ago. Although plywood typically lasts about 50 years under ideal circumstances, exposure to severe weather can shorten this lifespan. Laying new roofing on aging plywood may cause performance problems and potentially nullify manufacturer warranties, as roofing material producers often stipulate specific age and quality standards for the underlying structure.

Payne emphasizes that replacing worn-out plywood during re-roofing is a critical yet frequently neglected step. His Calgary team guarantees that the plywood they install during roof repairs will endure as long as the new shingles. No Payne Roofing's attention to both resilient shingles and sturdy underlying structures aligns with a broader industry initiative to address Alberta's increasingly harsh weather conditions.
