Research commissioned by the Energy & Climate Intelligence Unit (ECIU) reveals that extreme weather conditions have significantly impacted the mental health of farmers over the last five years. The report, compiled by Grounded Research with input from 300 farmers, indicates that nearly every British farmer (98%) has encountered severe weather during this period. Extreme rainfall (86%), drought (78%), and heatwaves (54%) were identified as the most frequently encountered phenomena.

Consequently, there has been a significant decline in mental health within the farming community. The research found that 92% of farmers experience anxiety, with 34% feeling "very anxious." Additionally, almost 60% reported experiencing feelings of depression, and 6% described themselves as "very depressed."

These sentiments stemmed from concerns over the potential loss of crops and livestock (78%), the expenses involved in recuperating from severe weather events (68%), and uncertainty regarding which crops to cultivate due to the erratic nature of the weather (52%). A significant 43% of farmers expressed concern that they might not earn sufficient income to sustain their farming operations during times of severe weather conditions.

The regularity of these issues weighing heavily on farmers' thoughts was notable, with approximately half (51%) expressing weekly anxiety over extreme weather, and nearly one in five (19%) experiencing this worry almost daily. Joanne Coates, a Yorkshire Dales beef farmer, noted that erratic weather conditions have increased hesitance among farmers to leave their properties, fearing they might need to swiftly address an unforeseen flood or other sudden emergencies. Ms Coates remarked, "This discourages individuals from engaging with community areas, further deepening isolation and loneliness."

Colin Chappell, a crop farmer from North Lincolnshire, remarked that weather conditions are pivotal to a farm's success or failure, adding that "This constant uncertainty takes a real toll on mental health." In spite of these significant impacts on their wellbeing, a spokesperson for the Royal Agricultural Beneultural Institution (Rabi) noted that their Big Farming Survey had earlier identified extreme and erratic weather as a principal contributor to stress and deteriorating mental health among those in the farming community. Rabi urges farmers facing challenges with their mental health or wellbeing to reach out to their toll-free helpline at 0800 188 4444.
