Intense rainfall throughout the past week has inundated southern and central Taiwan, delivering a setback to an area still recuperating from Typhoon Danas last month. The downpours, induced by the southwest monsoon, led to widespread floods, landslides, and road closures, severely damaging properties and prompting the evacuation of thousands. Continuous rain forced the closure of schools and offices in certain regions throughout the week.

The storm's fierce winds and heavy rainfall resulted in agricultural losses exceeding NT$2.6 billion (US$86.6 million), wreaked havoc on over 23,000 roofs, and toppled nearly 2,500 utility poles, causing widespread power failures. Several weather stations reported rainfall surpassing 100mm per hour, notably recording 122mm per hour in Pingtung County’s Sandimen Township.

During last month's heated mass recall campaigns, which coincided with the typhoon's impact, several lawmakers from the Chinese Nationalist Party (KMT) exploited the timing to rally support against the recalls. The KMT shared an image of Tainan Mayor Huang Wei-che (黃偉哲) from the Democratic Progressive Party (DPP) attending a recall event in June, accusing him of prioritizing recall efforts over disaster relief, though the photo was captured before the typhoon had developed. Taichung Mayor Lu Shiow-yen (盧秀燕) of the KMT also asserted at anti-recall gatherings that DPP lawmakers were solely preoccupied with the campaigns.

Despite these assertions, the president, premier, and local government officials were actively involved in visiting typhoon-affected regions. Chiayi Mayor Huang Min-hui (黃敏惠) of the KMT expressed gratitude to Kaohsiung Mayor Chen Chi-mai (陳其邁) of the DPP for swiftly dispatching personnel to assist Chiayi City in relief operations. Meanwhile, the KMT legislative caucus persisted in their critiques of the government's flood management initiatives. KMT Legislator Niu Hsu-ting (牛煦庭) criticized the sharing of photos depicting Taiwan Power Co workers repairing utility poles post-typhoon, labeling it as "emotional blackmail" of voters, especially since KMT lawmakers had previously reduced a subsidy for the utility. The unsuccessful recall attempts on July 26 highlighted the efficacy of the KMT's communication strategy.

The Central Weather Administration (CWA) shared a chart on Facebook illustrating the intensity of the brief yet intense rainfall that affected southern Taiwan. This chart connected hourly rainfall statistics with "common perceptions" to aid public comprehension of various rainfall intensities. For instance, it equated less than 0.1mm of rain per hour to “the refreshing mist from water sprayers at summer amusement parks.” The CWA characterized rainfall exceeding 80mm per hour as "creating a sensation akin to pressure or suffocation, as though the sky is falling; surpassing most urban drainage systems' capacities; and readily triggering floods and landslides." They noted this intensity is confined to severe weather events and might be a once-in-a-lifetime occurrence for many. The CWA's initiative to inform the public about scientific facts using clear and straightforward language exemplifies impartial public communication.

It is essential for the government to address the issue of misinformation earnestly and devise clear and swift methods to communicate facts and policies effectively, aiming to reduce the dissemination of damaging misinformation, not only from opposition groups but also from China. The KMT's use of biased narratives and misinformation undermines the public's ability to discern information critically. This communication must be woven throughout government channels to restore public confidence and fortify resilience.
