A Senate inquiry, backed by Labor, the coalition, and the Greens, has been approved to investigate a catastrophe that has claimed the lives of tens of thousands of marine creatures since its detection near the Fleurieu Peninsula in March.

The algal bloom has subsequently extended across the shoreline, leading to a collaborative $28 million investment package co-financed by the South Australian and federal governments. This scheme is aimed at clean-up, recovery, research, and industry assistance. On Tuesday, Premier Peter Malinauskas declared that the SA government would provide a matching $14 million commitment.

Following the Senate inquiry's approval on Wednesday evening, Nationals senator Ross Cadell remarked that the widespread backing indicated the matter transcended political boundaries. He stated, "Whether it be flood, drought, bushfire, or algal bloom, our communities need support, not politics." The investigation offers an opportunity to deeply understand the tangible consequences of this environmental catastrophe and evaluate the adequacy of the existing disaster relief funding framework.

The bloom has resulted in fish, dolphins, rays, and sharks washing up dead on beaches, alongside murky groundwater and a dense layer of foam on typically stunning beaches. The algal bloom was described as "unprecedented," leaving its duration and additional consequences uncertain, and underscoring the urgency for climate action.

The investment package, issued by federal Environment Minister Murray Watt, includes specific allocations. $8.5 million is dedicated to a coastal science and research network designed to enhance the early detection, monitoring, and surveillance of harmful algal bloom species. A $3 million rapid assessment will evaluate the effects of the bloom on fish stocks and fisheries. Additionally, a newly established $2 million national testing lab in SA will eliminate the necessity of sending samples to New Zealand for analysis.
