Super Typhoon Ragasa, recognized as the most formidable tropical cyclone of the year, struck on Wednesday, September 24, causing extensive devastation across the Philippines, Taiwan, and Hong Kong, and resulting in fatalities and injuries.

The super typhoon originated in the western Pacific, intensifying as sea temperatures rose. It eventually escalated to a Category 5 storm, the pinnacle classification on the Saffir–Simpson hurricane wind scale, exhibiting wind velocities of 260 km/h.

The event has prompted questions regarding the classification of such extreme weather phenomena. Scientific evidence indicates that human-induced climate warming, exacerbated by human activities, has been and continues to intensify these occurrences. An abundance of data confirms that reliance on fossil fuels is directly linked to the escalation of extreme weather phenomena.

