A recent report has revealed that the UK has lost more than 7 million tonnes in wheat output since 2020 due to severe weather conditions. This quantity is estimated by specialists at the Energy and Climate Intelligence Unit (ECIU) to be equivalent to a year's supply of bread, or over 4 billion loaves.

The shortfall has been driven by extreme weather events, intensified by climate change, including unprecedented heat, arid conditions, and extended spells of drought interspersed with intense, heavy rainfall capable of causing flooding. Farmers have also faced restrictions on irrigation and found it impossible to drill and manage crops due to extreme rainfall. This decade has witnessed two of the three most disastrous harvests on record, with 2020 being the most severe and 2024 ranking as the third worst.

These adverse conditions have diminished the UK's ability to independently produce its primary staple crop, leading to a decrease in wheat self-sufficiency from 96% in 2023 to 79% in 2024. Millers are increasingly relying on imports to compensate for reduced UK production, bringing in unprecedented quantities of wheat. Last autumn, wheat imports were twice the five-year average.

Tom Lancaster, an analyst specializing in land, food, and farming at the ECIU, commented: "This is the reality of farming amid a shifting climate. Adverse weather conditions are diminishing the British nature of our bread, as millers increasingly rely on imports to compensate for reduced UK production." He added that this trend is expected to worsen with the escalation of extreme weather in the years to come, leading to significant financial losses for British farmers, with billions in income evaporating.

Colin Chappell, an arable farmer from Lincolnshire, described the challenges: "I have never known a more challenging time to farm. As a farmer, I am used to managing the worst the weather has to throw at me, but recent years have become impossible." He noted that extended periods of heavy rainfall are succeeded by relentless droughts, with no respite, leaving him confronting a second consecutive disastrous harvest. Mr. Chappell stated he requires additional assistance to adapt to these extremes and build his business's resilience, but highlighted that "the closure of green farming initiatives in England means that such assistance is unavailable to me." He stressed that reopening these programs must be prioritized to ensure farmers can sustain the cultivation of wheat and other essential crops for the British populace.

The outlook for 2025 points to another challenging year for agricultural yields, with the drought experienced this year suggesting yet another disappointing harvest.
