A rapidly advancing wildfire has destroyed homes in Chinese Camp, a historic California Gold Rush settlement in Tuolumne County. The town was established around 1850 by Chinese miners who had been expelled from a neighboring camp. Initially named Camp Washington, it was later renamed to acknowledge the influx of thousands of Chinese settlers.

The blaze, identified by CalFire as the 6-5 Fire, was ignited by lightning. It is part of the TCU September Lightning Complex, which by Wednesday had expanded unchecked, engulfing over 19 square miles across various areas in Calaveras and Tuolumne counties. This particular fire remained uncontained as of early Wednesday.

A minimum of five homes were engulfed in flames, comprising a blend of standalone and mobile residences. A recreational vehicle on the premises also sustained damage. The rapidly advancing fire prompted the evacuation of Chinese Camp and nearby roads on Tuesday. On Tuesday evening, seven individuals were seen clearing large tree branches from a building and tossing sand onto flames, working to prevent the fire from spreading from an adjacent house. They continued their efforts for approximately half an hour until firefighters arrived.

Fire officials have called for more assets, including fire engines, bulldozers, and an aircraft. According to CalFire, this blaze is among over a dozen fires that ignited across California on Tuesday. No instant accounts of casualties or fatalities were available.

During the Gold Rush, thousands of Chinese immigrants migrated to California, where they encountered significant persecution. This included the imposition of an excessive Foreign Miners Tax, which was aimed at deterring them from engaging in mining activities.
