Hurricane Beryl, the inaugural major hurricane of the 2024 Atlantic season, set a record as the earliest Category 5 hurricane. As it passed approximately 150 km south of Barbados, the storm impacted the island as a Category 3 storm, causing large swells and waves along its south and west coasts.

While it caused only moderate harm to fundamental infrastructure and did not incite a humanitarian emergency, coastal assets sustained substantial damage. The total impact of the storm was valued at 193 million Barbados dollars, representing 0.15% of the nation’s GDP. Of this total, damage constituted 58%, losses comprised 36%, and additional costs accounted for 5%. The sectors of tourism, fisheries, agriculture, and the environment collectively represented 84% of these impacts.

This premature emergence and its repercussions on Barbados underscore the growing unpredictability of climate patterns, heightening the susceptibility of Caribbean small island developing States to severe weather phenomena. Consequently, it is crucial to consistently enhance all stages of disaster risk management, including risk assessment, prevention, mitigation, preparedness, and response, to empower nations and communities in effectively confronting the challenges posed by extreme weather and climate change.
