Mrutyunjay Mohapatra, Director of the India Meteorological Department (IMD), indicated that while light rain is expected to persist throughout the coming week, there is no forecast for heavy, widespread rainfall in the short term.

The earlier low-pressure system, which had caused heavy rainfall in coastal and western districts, has now moved towards north Telangana and Vidarbha, leading to a steady decrease in rainfall throughout the state.

Mohapatra highlighted the potential emergence of a new low-pressure system in the Bay of Bengal around September 20. He commented, "There is a chance that wind gusts may develop, though a cyclone is improbable at this time. The prediction should become more precise in the next few days," noting that the trajectory and strength of the system are contingent upon evolving meteorological conditions. He further assured that there is no imminent threat of cyclones or severe weather conditions to Odisha.

Despite the overall diminishing rainfall, the IMD cautions that localized heavy showers and thunderstorms remain a threat, especially in regions susceptible to flooding and low-lying zones. Authorities will continue to observe the Bay of Bengal for any potential events that might affect both coastal and interior regions.
