BOISE COUNTY, Idaho — State Highway 21 has reopened after adverse weather delayed rock scaling operations needed to address a major rockslide that occurred earlier this month.

The Idaho Transportation Department (ITD) declared on Tuesday that Highway 21 is now open from milepost 93.7 near Grandjean to milepost 105.5 at Banner Summit. The road was initially shut down on Dec. 9 due to a rockslide that occurred overnight between Sourdough and Banner Summit. It was accessible again on Dec. 12 with narrowed lanes and safety precautions established, but was subsequently shut once more for further rock scaling operations. On Friday, Dec. 19, ITD declared the road closure would persist over the weekend due to safety concerns arising from local weather conditions, which included strong winds and rainfall.

This section of road, known as "Avalanche Alley," shuts down multiple times each winter due to the threat of avalanches or when avalanches obstruct the route.

