Hurricane Melissa struck Jamaica on October 28, following a period of escalating forecasts and alerts regarding its unprecedented intensity. The storm experienced two separate phases of intensification, eventually reaching Category 5 hurricane status upon impact.

As an island nation, Jamaica faced significant challenges in Melissa's aftermath, including obstacles to relief initiatives and damage evaluation processes, with several regional airports out of service. Homes suffered damage or were completely destroyed, and communities and towns appeared drastically altered.

Fox Weather reported on satellite images captured by Vantor equipment, presenting a stark slideshow that exposed the devastation. Comparative "before" images showcased Jamaica's characteristic Caribbean blue waters and vibrant green scenery. Each "before" image was paired with an "after" shot of the identical location, revealing murky water, land devoid of foliage, and homes missing from the scene.

On October 28, Jamaican Prime Minister Andrew Holness (@andrewholnessjm) posted a segment of his conversation with CNN anchor Anderson Cooper, coupled with a message to his constituents. "Your homes might have suffered damage or been completely destroyed, and your communities and towns now appear different. I understand your suffering and share in your grief," Holness wrote.

Hurricane Melissa ranks among the most powerful hurricanes ever to strike the Caribbean, with its origins not being purely natural. According to Scientific American's report on October 28, the rise in average temperatures and the warming of ocean waters serve as catalysts for various extreme weather events, including hurricanes, floods, and wildfires. Melissa exemplified this phenomenon tragically, though extreme weather events were widespread in 2025. While hurricanes and other severe weather phenomena have long been a part of our environment, the ongoing rise in temperatures results in orders of magnitude more damage and is deadlier than before, resembling adding fuel to an already blazing inferno.

With the increasing frequency of extreme weather events, keeping abreast of crucial climate matters is vital for comprehending and maneuvering through them. People in hurricane-prone areas can stay prepared by keeping a "go bag" ready, gathering essentials such as batteries and water, and always avoiding floodwaters when they surge.
