Global communities are increasingly encountering the impacts of climbing temperatures, leading to a surge in insurance claims linked to intensified storms.

Suzi Bahan, who ran a modest tiki bar in Fort Myers Beach, saw her establishment ruined by Hurricane Ian. "Crossing that bridge and witnessing the remnants of my existence brought me to my knees. It was life-altering," Bahan stated. She had anticipated recovery with the backing of two insurance providers; while one compensated her promptly, the second failed to pay, leading to a lawsuit appeal.

Bahan's situation is not unique, as numerous individuals struggle to receive insurance payouts after disasters. A 2024 publication by the Professional Law Group reported that between 26% and 50% of insurance claims in Florida were rejected following hurricanes during the 2023-2024 season. This challenge extends beyond hurricanes; Californians, for instance, face escalating insurance premiums due to the growing danger of wildfires. Certain insurance firms are also quietly raising deductibles to offset the rise in claims.

