TUOLUMNE COUNTY, Calif. (AP) — A rapidly advancing wildfire consumed homes in the California Gold Rush town of Chinese Camp, originally established around 1850 by Chinese miners who had been expelled from a neighboring camp.

The blaze swiftly grew to encompass 10 square miles, prompting the evacuation of Chinese Camp and nearby highways on Tuesday, as reported by CalFire, the state's primary firefighting agency. There were no immediate reports of injuries or fatalities. By Wednesday morning, containment efforts had not been successful, and the blaze continued to expand unchecked.

A minimum of five residences, comprising a combination of standalone and mobile homes, were ablaze in the rural area. On Tuesday evening, outside a residence, a desperate attempt was made to prevent the blaze from spreading from a neighboring house. A group of seven individuals relocated large tree branches and shoveled sand onto the flames for approximately 30 minutes until firefighters arrived. An RV on the property was damaged by the fire.

Fire authorities have called for more resources, including fire engines, bulldozers, and an aircraft.

CalFire reported that the fire, ignited by lightning, is among over a dozen that ignited across California on Tuesday. This blaze is part of several known as the TCU September Lightning Complex, which has consumed over 19 square miles across various areas.

The Gold Rush town of Chinese Camp was established by Chinese miners after they were expelled from a neighboring settlement, as noted by Visit Tuolumne County. Initially named Camp Washington, it was later renamed to honor the influx of thousands of Chinese settlers. During the Gold Rush, Chinese immigrants faced widespread discrimination and hostility, including the imposition of a steep Foreign Miners Tax intended to force them out of the mining industry.
