The Environment Agency has completed 151 flood schemes across England in the 12 months since Labour came into power in July last year. These initiatives, which included the construction of new defenses and upgrades to existing ones, have significantly enhanced flood protection for over 24,000 homes and businesses nationwide.

Among the completed projects are the Saltfleet to Gibraltar Point Beach Management scheme in Lincolnshire, designed to protect 2,904 local properties, and the Cockett Wick seawall improvement scheme in Jaywick, which safeguards 1,526 properties. Additionally, work concluded in July on the £75 million Canvey Island Flood Defence Scheme in Essex, renewing three kilometres of tidal defences to protect more than 6,000 properties for the next 50 years.

Ministers state that the broader investment strategy is set to benefit more than 890,000 properties, protecting businesses, safeguarding farmland, and supporting the Government's plans to build 1.5 million new homes by 2029. The Government has pledged £2.65 billion for flood defence spending over the next two years until April 2026, representing a £250 million increase over the previous allocation. Further commitments include £7.9 billion for flood defences from 2026 to 2036, bringing the total investment to more than £10.5 billion over the 12 years from 2024. This investment is also expected to help towns and cities prosper by driving economic growth and creating new jobs.

The Environment Agency highlights that the number of properties in England at risk from flooding is increasing due to rising sea levels, more volatile rainfall patterns, outdated flood defences, and increased development projects. Currently, 6.3 million properties in England are at risk, a figure estimated to rise to around eight million (one in four properties) by 2050. Caroline Douglass, executive director of floods and coastal risk management at the Environment Agency, stated, “Flooding has a devastating impact on communities across the country and will continue to worsen as extreme weather is caused by our changing climate. Our teams and partners have worked to deliver another 151 flood schemes that are vitally needed across the country – and are ready to take action to protect people and homes this winter. We will continue to work with the government to deliver its long-term flood investment programme, ensuring the most vulnerable communities get the protection they need.”
